<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonine Wall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">142</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Antonine Wall was a Roman turf rampart built across the Forth–Clyde isthmus of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from 142 on the orders of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a>. It marked the northern frontier of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for about twenty years before the army withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. It crossed the land of the Damnonii and is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonine-wall.jpg" alt="Antonine Wall" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Created by NormanEinstein, September 20, 2005; derivative work by Talifero · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadrians_Wall_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
